---
.title = "Desktop: Linux, Windows, macOS",
.description = "What an Oriel app runs on and needs on Linux (GTK 4 and WebKitGTK), Windows (Win32 and WebView2) and macOS (AppKit and WKWebView), with each platform's options and limits.",
.date = .date("2026-10-12T00:00:00"),
.authors = ["Oriel"],
.layout = "docs.shtml",
---

On the desktop an Oriel app is one native executable: Oriel's platform-neutral
core (`src/core/`: the app, windows, IPC, security) on top of a thin shell
per operating system (`src/platform/linux`, `windows`, `macos`) that drives
the system's own web view. Nothing is bundled: no browser engine, no
runtime. The same `main.zig` and frontend build for all three, and every
module and plugin has a backend on each (with the few exceptions below).

This page says what each desktop platform uses underneath, what it needs to
build and run, its build options and its limits. Building installers is on
[Packaging]($link.page('docs/packaging')), signing on
[Signing]($link.page('docs/signing')); phones are on
[Android]($link.page('docs/android')) and [iOS]($link.page('docs/ios')).

# [Availability]($heading.id('availability'))

| | Linux | Windows | macOS |
|---|---|---|---|
| Window and app | GTK 4 (`GtkApplication`) | Win32 | AppKit (`NSApplication`) |
| Web view | WebKitGTK 6.0 | Microsoft Edge WebView2 (Evergreen) | `WKWebView` |
| CPUs | x86_64, aarch64 | x86_64, aarch64 | Apple silicon, Intel |
| Oldest OS | GTK 4.12+ with WebKitGTK 6.0 (Ubuntu 24.04, Debian 13, Fedora 39) | Windows 10 1809 (MSIX minimum), with the WebView2 runtime | macOS 13 (15 with Swift sources) |
| Built on | Linux | Windows, or cross-compiled from Linux | A Mac (Xcode or the Command Line Tools) |
| Packages | deb, rpm, AppImage | NSIS `setup.exe`, MSIX | `.app`, `.dmg` |
| Platform options | none | `.windows.capabilities` | `.macos.usage_descriptions`, `.macos.entitlements`, `.macos.hooks` |

Modules that don't exist on a desktop platform resolve to
`oriel.<module>.available = false` there ([Modules]($link.page('docs/modules'))):
`system_ai` everywhere on the desktop, and `layer_shell` (a Wayland feature)
on Windows and macOS.

# [Quick start]($heading.id('quick-start'))

Check and install what the platform needs (without administrator rights
where it can):

```sh
oriel doctor --fix
```

Then, on each platform:

```sh
oriel dev          # run against the dev server with hot reload
oriel build        # production build: zig-out/bin/<name> (macOS: also zig-out/<Name>.app)
oriel package      # installers for this platform (Packaging)
```

Cross-compile Windows from Linux:

```sh
oriel build -Dtarget=x86_64-windows       # zig-out/bin/<name>.exe
oriel package -Dtarget=x86_64-windows     # zig-out/package/<name>-<version>-setup.exe
```

# [How it works]($heading.id('how-it-works'))

>[]($block.attrs("mermaid"))
```
flowchart TB
  Core["src/core: App, windows, IPC,<br/>security, modules"] -->|"platform contract<br/>(src/platform/platform.zig)"| Shells
  subgraph Shells["Shell per OS"]
    L["Linux<br/>GtkApplication + WebKitWebView"]
    W["Windows<br/>Win32 window + WebView2"]
    M["macOS<br/>NSApplication + WKWebView"]
  end
  L -->|"app scheme handler,<br/>script message handlers"| P1["Page"]
  W -->|"app.localhost request filter,<br/>postMessage"| P2["Page"]
  M -->|"app:// WKURLSchemeHandler,<br/>WKScriptMessageHandlerWithReply"| P3["Page"]
```

`src/platform/platform.zig` picks the shell at compile time from the target
and checks that it exports every declaration the core uses (windows, the
main-thread queue, evaluating script, quitting). Each shell runs the
system's event loop on the main thread, serves the embedded frontend through
a custom scheme with the app's CSP headers, installs the one bridge script
(`src/core/bridge_js.zig`) and carries IPC with the same token, origin and
isolation checks; sync commands run on the main loop, async ones on the
worker pool ([Commands]($link.page('docs/commands'))).

**Linux** (`src/platform/linux/`). `Shell.zig` runs a `GtkApplication`:
the event loop, the menu bar, SIGTERM/SIGINT as a clean quit, and logind's
`PrepareForSleep` as `oriel.lifecycle`'s `suspend` / `resume`. GApplication
hands later launches to the running instance over D-Bus (single instance,
deep links). `window.zig` makes a `GtkApplicationWindow` with a
`WebKitWebView` and its navigation policy; `scheme.zig` serves `app://`;
`bridge.zig` uses WebKit's script message handlers; `dev_server.zig` starts
the dev server as a child that dies with the app. On NVIDIA's proprietary
driver Oriel sets `WEBKIT_DISABLE_DMABUF_RENDERER=1` (unless already set),
which avoids WebKitGTK's busy compositing loop there. `overlay.zig` places
overlay windows (below).

**Windows** (`src/platform/windows/`). A Win32 message loop and windows
(`win32.zig`, `window.zig`), with per-monitor DPI awareness (v2). WebView2 is
reached through hand-declared COM vtables (`webview2.zig`); its environment
comes from `WebView2Loader.dll`, loaded only from the executable's own
directory (no DLL search-order hijacking), with user data in
`%LOCALAPPDATA%\<app id>\WebView2`. Assets are served at
`https://app.localhost/` through `AddWebResourceRequestedFilter`; IPC goes
over `chrome.webview.postMessage` and `WebMessageReceived`. A named mutex
makes the app single-instance: a second launch finds the running one's
hidden host window and sends it its arguments (`WM_COPYDATA`), then quits.
`oriel.lifecycle`'s `terminating` comes at `WM_ENDSESSION`.

**macOS** (`src/platform/macos/`). AppKit through the Objective-C runtime,
declared in plain Zig without SDK headers (`src/platform/apple/objc.zig`,
shared with iOS, so `zig build check -Dtarget=aarch64-macos` runs on any
host): `Shell.zig` runs
`NSApplication` with main-thread tasks on the GCD main queue, a default
app / Edit / Window menu bar, SIGTERM/SIGINT as a clean quit, and a Dock
click that reopens a hidden main window. `window.zig` makes `NSWindow`s with
a `WKWebView` and its navigation and UI delegates (JavaScript `alert` /
`confirm` / `prompt` are `NSAlert` sheets); `apple/scheme.zig` serves
`app://` through a `WKURLSchemeHandler` and `apple/bridge.zig` uses a
`WKScriptMessageHandlerWithReply`, the same code as on iOS. Launch Services sends a bundled app's
second launch to the running one; `single_instance.zig` also catches
binaries run directly (a terminal, `open -n`) with an `flock` and a Unix
socket in the per-user temp directory when the app sets
`on_second_instance`.

Native code of your own reaches these objects through
[platform hooks]($link.page('docs/platform-hooks')) (`oriel.hooks` hands
your Zig the `GtkWindow*` / `WebKitWebView*`, `HWND` / `ICoreWebView2*`,
`NSWindow*` / `WKWebView*`).

# [Examples]($heading.id('examples'))

## [An overlay window on Wayland (Linux)]($heading.id('example-overlay'))

```zig
// build.zig: compile the layer-shell support in (the library loads at run time).
const dep = b.dependency("oriel", .{ .target = target, .optimize = optimize, .layer_shell = true });
```

Windows that ask for `always_on_top`, `skip_taskbar` or a `placement`
([Windows]($link.page('docs/windows'))) then become layer surfaces on
compositors with wlr-layer-shell (Hyprland, Sway, KDE...). On X11 the same
options use EWMH hints and need nothing extra.

## [A Bluetooth device app in an MSIX package (Windows)]($heading.id('example-msix'))

```zig
.permissions = .{ .bluetooth = "Connects to your heart-rate strap" }, // <DeviceCapability Name="bluetooth"/>
.windows = .{ .capabilities = &.{
    .{ .name = "humaninterfacedevice", .kind = .device },
} },
```

## [Entitlements and usage keys (macOS)]($heading.id('example-macos'))

```zig
.permissions = .{ .camera = "Scans receipts" }, // NSCameraUsageDescription + com.apple.security.device.camera
.macos = .{
    .usage_descriptions = &.{.{ .key = "NSAppleEventsUsageDescription", .text = "Controls Music" }},
    .entitlements = &.{"com.apple.security.automation.apple-events"},
},
```

# [Reference]($heading.id('reference'))

## [Linux]($heading.id('linux'))

**Build requirements:** Zig 0.16 (the CLI installs it), GTK 4 and
WebKitGTK 6.0 development packages:

| Distribution | Packages |
|---|---|
| Arch | `gtk4 webkitgtk-6.0` |
| Debian, Ubuntu | `libgtk-4-dev libwebkitgtk-6.0-dev` |
| Fedora | `gtk4-devel webkitgtk6.0-devel` |

**Run requirements:** GTK 4.12+ and WebKitGTK 6.0. gtk4-layer-shell is
optional (overlays on Wayland).

**Wayland, X11 and overlays** (`src/platform/linux/overlay.zig`):

| Session | `always_on_top`, `skip_taskbar`, `placement` | Transparency, click-through |
|---|---|---|
| Wayland with gtk4-layer-shell and `-Dlayer_shell=true` | Layer surfaces (overlay layer, anchors and margins); dragging moves the surface by its margins | Yes |
| X11 | EWMH hints (`_NET_WM_STATE_ABOVE`, skip taskbar) and `XMoveWindow`, applied when the window maps; Xlib is looked up at run time | Yes |
| Wayland without layer-shell | Ignored (logged once): a normal Wayland window can't do them | Yes |

gtk4-layer-shell must be loaded before GTK, so on Wayland an app built with
`-Dlayer_shell=true` restarts itself once with `libgtk4-layer-shell.so.0`
in `LD_PRELOAD` when the library is installed (nothing links it, so the app
also runs without it). `ORIEL_NO_LAYER_SHELL=1` skips that.

**Platform options:** none. Linux packages declare no permissions.

## [Windows]($heading.id('windows-platform'))

**Requirements:**

- The WebView2 runtime (built into Windows 11 and current Windows 10; the
  NSIS installer checks for it).
- `WebView2Loader.dll` next to the executable. The CLI downloads it from
  NuGet, checks its SHA-512 against NuGet's catalog, caches it, and passes
  `-Dwebview2-loader=<cached dll>` when building for Windows (`oriel webview2`
  manages the cache). An explicit `-Dwebview2-loader=<path>` wins;
  `ORIEL_NO_WEBVIEW2_FETCH=1` turns the download off. Offline: take
  `runtimes/win-x64/native/WebView2Loader.dll` (or `win-arm64`) out of the
  `Microsoft.Web.WebView2` `.nupkg` (a ZIP). Apps built with `-Dnative_ui`
  have no web view and don't need it.
- NSIS (`makensis`) for the installer: `oriel setup nsis`, or found in
  Program Files.
- Node.js for Vite templates (`oriel setup node`).

**Platform options:**

```zig
.windows = .{ .capabilities = &.{
    .{ .name = "bluetooth", .kind = .device },
} },
```

| `Capability` field | Type | Default | Meaning |
|---|---|---|---|
| `name` | `[]const u8` | required | `[A-Za-z0-9._-]`, or a device interface's `{GUID}`; up to 200 characters. |
| `kind` | `enum { general, uap, restricted, device }` | `.general` | `<Capability>`, `<uap:Capability>`, `<rescap:Capability>` (restricted: Store review), `<DeviceCapability>`. |

They join the capabilities the declared permissions write
(`microphone`, `webcam`, `location`, `bluetooth` device capabilities,
`privateNetworkClientServer` for `local_network`, the graphics-capture
restricted ones for `screen_capture`), plus `internetClient` and
`runFullTrust`, each once. Only the MSIX package has capabilities; the NSIS
installer ignores them. An invalid name is a build error.

**Installers.** The NSIS installer is per-user
(`%LOCALAPPDATA%\Programs\<name>`, no administrator rights), adds Start Menu
shortcuts and an uninstaller, and supports silent `setup.exe /S`. MSIX
targets Windows 10.0.17763.0 by default (`.package.msix.min_version`).
Details: [Packaging]($link.page('docs/packaging')).

## [macOS]($heading.id('macos-platform'))

**Requirements:** a Mac with Xcode or the Command Line Tools
(`xcode-select --install`). The macOS shell links AppKit and WebKit from the
SDK, so macOS builds are made on a Mac; cross-building from another OS isn't
supported.

**Deployment target.** A target without a version (`oriel build`, or
`-Dtarget=aarch64-macos`) builds for macOS 13, so a build from a newer Mac
runs on older ones; pass `oriel.resolveTarget(b, ...)`'s target to every
executable you add. An explicit `-Dtarget=aarch64-macos.14.0` wins. Swift
sources in `.macos.hooks` or an extension's `.macos.objc` raise it to 15.

**Bundles.** `oriel build` also writes `zig-out/<Name>.app`; `oriel package`
builds `.app` and `.dmg`. Start the bundle (`open zig-out/<Name>.app`) to
get notifications through Notification Center, permission prompts under the
app's name, and deep links.

**Platform options:**

| Field | Type | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `usage_descriptions` | `[]const UsageDescription` | `Info.plist` usage keys no `.permissions` kind writes: `.{ .key, .text }`, the key ending in `UsageDescription` (`[A-Za-z0-9_.~-]`), the text not empty. A key a kind writes too takes this text. |
| `entitlements` | `[]const []const u8` | Boolean hardened-runtime entitlements (`[A-Za-z0-9_.~-]`) added to `<Name>.entitlements`, used when signing with a Developer ID. The declared permissions add theirs (`device.audio-input` for microphone and system audio, `device.camera`, `personal-information.location`). |
| `hooks` | `[]const PlatformHook` | Objective-C or Swift [platform hooks]($link.page('docs/platform-hooks').ref('apple-declare')): the app delegate, windows and WebKit delegates; at most 16. Their sources need the macOS SDK: `-Dmacos_sdk=<MacOSX.sdk>`, else `$SDKROOT` when it is a MacOSX SDK, else `xcrun`. |

**Permissions are per bundle.** macOS attributes an unbundled binary to the
app that started it (Terminal, an IDE), so test microphone, accessibility
and screen capture from an `.app`. An Accessibility grant is tied to the
exact code signature: after rebuilding an ad-hoc-signed `.app`, macOS may
keep matching the old build ("Failed to match existing code requirement" in
tccd's log) even after removing and re-adding it. Test input injection from
a Developer ID-signed bundle, or from a terminal that already has
Accessibility. Microphone, system audio and notifications work with ad-hoc
bundles.

`ORIEL_SNAPSHOT=/tmp/shot.png` saves the main window's page (WebKit's
snapshot API) a second after it loaded, without a Screen Recording grant.

## [Single instance]($heading.id('single-instance'))

With `App.Config.on_second_instance` set (or the `deep_link` module on), a
second launch hands its arguments to the running instance and exits:

| | How |
|---|---|
| Linux | GApplication's command line over D-Bus |
| Windows | A named mutex; the arguments go to the running instance's host window with `WM_COPYDATA` (up to 3 s to find it) |
| macOS | Launch Services sends a bundle's second launch to the running one (a reopen). With `on_second_instance`, also an `flock` on `<user temp dir>/oriel-<id>.lock` and a Unix socket, for binaries run directly (arguments as a JSON array up to 64 KiB, peers' uids checked, 2 s timeouts) |

Without `on_second_instance` the running instance shows its main window.
See [Lifecycle]($link.page('docs/lifecycle')) and
[Deep links]($link.page('docs/deep-links')).

## [Where things go]($heading.id('locations'))

| | Linux | Windows | macOS |
|---|---|---|---|
| Log file | `$XDG_DATA_HOME/<id>/app.log` | `%LOCALAPPDATA%\<id>\app.log` | `~/Library/Logs/<id>/app.log` |
| App data and cache | [Paths]($link.page('docs/paths')) | [Paths]($link.page('docs/paths')) | [Paths]($link.page('docs/paths')) |

## [Limits]($heading.id('limits'))

- **Linux:** Wayland compositors don't let apps position normal windows or
  keep them on top: overlays need layer-shell (above). Window positions
  apply on X11.
- **Windows:** WebView2 shows a cross-origin error page inside a frame whose
  navigation Oriel blocked (the navigation is still blocked).
- **macOS:** builds need a Mac; permissions follow the bundle's signature
  (above).
- `system_ai` has no desktop backend yet; `layer_shell` is Linux only.

# [Troubleshooting]($heading.id('troubleshooting'))

- **Linux: the build can't find `gtk4` or `webkitgtk-6.0`**: install the
  development packages above; `oriel doctor` names what's missing.
- **Linux: high CPU on NVIDIA**: Oriel already sets
  `WEBKIT_DISABLE_DMABUF_RENDERER=1` there; if you set it to `0` yourself,
  remove that.
- **Linux: `always_on_top/skip_taskbar/placement need layer-shell on
  Wayland`**: build with `-Dlayer_shell=true` and install gtk4-layer-shell, or
  run under X11.
- **Windows: `WebView2Loader.dll not found next to <exe>`**: package with
  `-Dwebview2-loader=...` (the CLI does it), or copy the DLL next to the
  executable.
- **Windows: `info: no cached WebView2Loader.dll and ORIEL_NO_WEBVIEW2_FETCH=1`**:
  unset the variable or pass `-Dwebview2-loader=<path>`.
- **macOS: `macOS 13.0 is below what Swift supports here`**: Swift hooks or
  extensions need macOS 15; drop the explicit lower version.
- **macOS: a permission prompt never appears**: run the `.app`, not the bare
  binary.

